---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';
---

<Base title="Editorial policy" description="How Data Governance Signal selects, writes, reviews and corrects its content.">
  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Trust</p>
      <h1>Editorial policy</h1>
      <p>Our value depends on being trusted. These are the rules we work by.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>1. Every item has a source</h2>
      <p>
        We always link to the original. Where possible we cite the primary source — the regulator's decision, the
        official journal, the standard, the company's own announcement — and mark it as such. When we rely on a
        secondary source or an interested party, we say so.
      </p>
      <h2>2. A human editor approves everything</h2>
      <p>
        AI helps us monitor sources, assess relevance and draft summaries. A human editor reviews every draft against
        its sources and then edits, approves or rejects it. Nothing is published automatically.
      </p>
      <h2>3. We are transparent about AI</h2>
      <p>
        Every article states that it was prepared with AI assistance and reviewed by the editor. The editor is
        responsible for what we publish.
      </p>
      <h2>4. We write our own summaries</h2>
      <p>
        We summarise in our own words and keep summaries short. We do not republish full articles or third-party images.
        If you want the full story, follow the source link.
      </p>
      <h2>5. Importance is an editorial judgement</h2>
      <p>
        Importance ratings (1–5) reflect our assessment of the impact on data governance practice. They are never for
        sale and are not influenced by advertisers, sponsors or vendors.
      </p>
      <h2>6. Commercial content is always labelled</h2>
      <p>
        If we carry sponsorships or other commercial content, it will be clearly labelled as such and kept separate from
        editorial content. Sponsors have no influence on what we cover or how.
      </p>
      <h2>7. Vendors are judged by impact, not marketing</h2>
      <p>
        Vendor news is covered when it changes something for practitioners — a significant launch, acquisition or
        research finding. Vendor performance claims are attributed to the vendor.
      </p>
      <h2>8. We correct mistakes openly</h2>
      <p>
        If we get something wrong, we fix it and add a visible correction note to the article stating what changed and
        when. Report errors to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .narrow {
    max-width: calc(var(--measure) + 2 * var(--gutter));
  }
  .prose h2 {
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    margin: 2rem 0 0.6rem;
  }
</style>
